**Theorem 7.2 (component intervals and a sufficient coverage condition).**
For the complete contact polytope,
$U_m=\bigcup_{C\text{ component of }\mathcal H_m}J(C)$.
In particular, deleting from the edge graph only the edges with
nine occupied groups leaves $\mathcal H_8$. If one remaining
component contains vertices attaining both $r_-$ and $r_+$,
then $U_8=[r_-,r_+]$. The existence of such a component is a
sufficient condition, not a necessary condition imposed by the
count criterion. The exact coverage condition is coverage by the
union of the component intervals, which may overlap even for
disconnected components.

Proof. A connected finite graph with its geometric edges has
connected continuous image under $K$; the image is exactly the
interval between the minimum and maximum vertex values. This
also holds for an isolated vertex. Taking the union and using
(FC.15) proves the first assertion. The vertex and edge bounds in
Theorem 6.1 show that all vertices are retained for $m=8$ and the
only removed edges have nine groups. A component containing both
global extrema has the entire required interval as its image.
Conversely interval coverage only asks that the union of the
$J(C)$ cover that interval; it does not ask those components to
be connected to one another. In particular the criterion includes
coverage assembled from overlapping component images and the
constant-$K$ case. $\square$

